Deep Literature Review on Evaluating Event-Centric Knowledge Graphs
Scope and definitions
Event-centric knowledge graphs (EKGs, sometimes “event knowledge graphs” or “event-centric KGs”) represent events as first-class nodes and organize surrounding information—participants, locations, temporal anchors, and inter-event dependencies—around those events rather than around static entity facts. This design choice is motivated by the observation that many real-world phenomena are inherently temporal and change-oriented, and that event-centered modeling helps express dynamic domain aspects and link people, places, actions, and objects in a way that plain entity–entity triples often cannot. [1]
A key consequence for evaluation is that “EKG quality” is not only about correctness of extracted triples, but also about whether the graph faithfully represents event structure (what happened), event grounding (who/where/when), and event logic (how events relate temporally and causally). Event modeling standards and conventions show why these dimensions are hard to collapse into a single “accuracy” metric. For instance, the Simple Event Model (SEM) was designed with minimal semantic commitment to stay interoperable across domains while still providing a usable schema for actors, places, and time; this flexibility is powerful, but it also means that completeness and correctness are partly “fit-for-purpose” and context dependent. [2]
Temporal modeling in text and extracted event representations further complicates evaluation because events can be anchored explicitly or only inferentially; temporal relations can be underspecified; and equivalent temporal structures may be expressed by different but logically consistent edge sets. TimeML became influential precisely because it offers a rich specification for event and temporal expressions and their ordering relations—highlighting that temporal evaluation is not just matching timestamps but reasoning over structured temporal constraints. [3]
Ecosystem of event-centric knowledge graphs and benchmarks
A central driver of EKG research has been the creation of large, reusable resources integrating event knowledge from heterogeneous sources. EventKG is a prominent example: it integrates event-centric information and temporal relations from multiple reference sources and emphasizes canonical representation, provenance, and multilingual coverage; it also explicitly frames its contribution as improving coverage and completeness of event representations compared to its sources. [4] In the EventKG work, large-scale sources such as Wikidata[5] and DBpedia[6] are used as reference knowledge bases together with semi-structured event sources, in order to make event and temporal knowledge more directly usable via RDF/SPARQL. [7]
Other EKG-like resources focus less on encyclopedic events and more on event/process knowledge extracted from text at massive scale. ASER (“Activities, States, Events, and their Relations”) is an eventuality-centric knowledge graph built from very large text corpora, emphasizing syntactic pattern extraction and a typed relation inventory influenced by discourse relation schemes; it positions its evaluation as both intrinsic (human validation of extraction outputs) and extrinsic (utility in downstream reasoning tasks). [8]
A distinct strand emphasizes cross-lingual access and multi-dataset integration for broader analytics. OEKG is presented as a multilingual event-centric temporal KG composed by integrating seven datasets (spanning question answering, entity recommendation, and NER) through named-graph-based integration and linking to EventKG, with demonstrations via practical use cases such as hybrid QA over KG + news and language-specific event recommendation. [9]
Event-centric graphs also arise from IE pipelines over news and specialized domains. The NewsReader line of work (“building event-centric knowledge graphs from news”) defines ECKGs as graphs where information is related to events to give a temporal dimension; in their quality assessment, the authors explicitly describe the lack of a proper gold standard and therefore rely on human judgment over sampled triples. [10] Domain-specific spatio-temporal KGs such as AugGKG (a grid-augmented geographic knowledge graph) highlight another evaluation angle: supporting complex spatio-temporal queries and assessing efficiency/completeness/accuracy under those query workloads—important because many EKG applications are query- and reasoning-driven rather than “extract exactly these edges.” [11]
Benchmarks for evaluation often come from neighboring “event understanding” tasks rather than from “EKG evaluation” per se. Event-centric question answering datasets such as Event-QA target complex SPARQL queries over EventKG and provide multilingual verbalizations, enabling QA-based evaluation that probes whether event/entity relations and temporal constraints are represented in a way that a system can exploit. [12] For temporal IE and temporal relation extraction (fundamental sub-skills for temporal EKG edges), the TempEval family and QA TempEval are influential: QA TempEval explicitly reframes evaluation around question answering performance, arguing that QA better captures task-relevant temporal understanding than uniformly scoring all annotated relations. [13]
Causality is often evaluated through dedicated annotated resources and shared tasks rather than through “EKGs” directly. Causal-TimeBank work provides an annotation framework inspired by TimeML and a dataset covering temporal and causal relations, used to build and evaluate causal link extraction. [14] The Event StoryLine Corpus introduces storyline extraction as a benchmark spanning documents over time and supports evaluation of temporal and causal relation detection in story contexts. [15] Multilingual causality benchmarks like MECI and finance-focused shared tasks like FinCausal 2020 reflect the growing demand for multilingual and domain-specific causal evaluation. [16]
Finally, from 2025–2026 there is visible momentum toward LLM-centered or LLM-assisted event-structure evaluation frameworks. The STAGE benchmark (2026) evaluates event-centric narrative understanding using complementary tasks, including a dedicated Event-Structure Consistency (ESC) evaluation that decomposes event summary quality into coverage and faithfulness, explicitly acknowledging that event abstraction is inherently non-symmetric and cannot be fully evaluated by matching to a fixed event inventory. [17]
Knowledge graph evaluation foundations: intrinsic, extrinsic, and scalable auditing
EKG evaluation inherits much of the broader KG quality literature. Surveys on KG quality control and quality management emphasize that “quality” is multi-dimensional and that evaluation must be connected to construction processes and improvement workflows. One survey explicitly states it defines “six main evaluation dimensions of KG quality” and studies their correlations, framing evaluation as part of “quality control” and “quality enhancement.” [18] A more recent comprehensive survey on KG quality management emphasizes recurring issues—incorrect/outdated entries, incompleteness, noise, and open-world semantics—and argues for end-to-end quality management spanning assessment, detection, correction, and completion. [19] These general findings matter for EKGs because EKGs are typically dynamic (new events, corrected times/locations, evolving narratives) and operate under open-world assumptions, making naïve “missing = false” completeness notions unreliable. [20]
From a methods perspective, KG evaluation research offers several building blocks relevant to EKGs:
Constraint and schema validation is a natural “baseline” layer: W3C[21] SHACL is a standard language for validating RDF graphs against shapes (constraints), formalizing a practical route to syntactic/structural validity checks (cardinalities, datatypes, required properties, etc.) that can be automated and repeatedly applied. [22] Test-driven linked data quality assessment complements this by framing quality checks as test cases (often SPARQL-based), inspired by software testing, and reporting large-scale assessment across datasets using reusable query templates. [23] Frameworks like Luzzu further institutionalize this “metric plug-in + scalable assessment” approach for linked data quality, emphasizing extensibility and streaming evaluation. [24]
Scalable human-in-the-loop auditing is another key building block. Efficient KG accuracy evaluation work (PVLDB) studies statistically meaningful accuracy estimation under human annotation budgets and introduces sampling designs (including extensions to evolving KGs) that can dramatically reduce annotation costs while retaining evaluation quality. [25] This matters for EKGs because gold-standard full labeling of temporal/causal/event-evolution structures is typically infeasible at scale; sampling-based auditing is often the only realistic option for ongoing quality monitoring. [26]
Task-based (extrinsic) evaluation has become increasingly important as KG researchers recognize that intrinsic metrics (triple-level correctness/completeness) may not predict downstream utility. KGrEaT is an explicit framework proposal: it evaluates KGs via downstream tasks (classification, clustering, recommendation, etc.), using fixed task setups and entity mapping to compare graphs as “inputs,” and it highlights that accessibility/contextualization (labels/descriptions) can heavily influence downstream mapping and thus measured utility. [27] This “evaluate the graph by what it enables” paradigm aligns naturally with EKG applications like timeline generation, event-centric QA, narrative reconstruction, and risk monitoring—use cases where missing a few edges may be less harmful than misrepresenting causal order or temporal scope. [28]
Recent work also explores LLM-assisted validation and evaluation for KGs. For example, KGValidator proposes an LLM-based method for validating KG completion outputs to reduce the need for manual labeling, and other frameworks explicitly combine LLM and human feedback loops for KG optimization and validation. [29] While these are not EKG-specific, they are relevant because event triples are often extracted from text and exist precisely at the boundary between structured constraints and linguistic ambiguity. [30]
Current EKG evaluation practices in the literature
Across the EKG literature, evaluation methods cluster into several recurring patterns—each valuable, but often partial.
Triple-level precision/recall/F1 against human annotation is common when the goal is relationship extraction or event threading. Early work on event evolution graphs defines event evolution graphs as directed graphs whose vertices are events and edges encode evolutions, and it evaluates extracted evolution relationships using precision and recall against manually annotated relations. [31] This paradigm remains influential in later event evolution and event relation extraction research because it is straightforward and comparable to classic IE evaluation, but it is inherently limited by annotation scope and by the fact that equivalent high-level event structures can map to multiple valid edge sets. [32]
Coverage/completeness and attribute-level correctness dominate evaluations for integrated encyclopedic EKGs. EventKG illustrates a typical pattern: it reports coverage and completeness over event representations and compares the presence of time and location attributes across sources; later, it evaluates event identification, time fusion, and location fusion using user annotations over sampled events, reporting correctness, missingness, and derived precision for conflicting time information and for locations (including an extended inference variant). [33] Importantly, this style of evaluation validates key EKG primitives (eventness, dates, places) while leaving many “EKG-defining” higher-order properties—such as causality chain quality or multi-hop reasoning support—largely unmeasured. [34]
Crowdsourced or expert human judgment is frequently used where no gold standard exists. NewsReader-style ECKGs explicitly state they lacked a proper gold standard and therefore used human judgment over randomly sampled event triples. [10] ASER uses a more formal crowdsourcing protocol: sampling extracted eventualities and asking annotators to judge whether the extracted eventuality captures the meaning of its originating sentence; it also compares ASER’s knowledge to ConceptNet and evaluates utility on a downstream commonsense reasoning benchmark (Winograd Schema Challenge). [35]
Temporal-specific evaluation has developed beyond plain P/R/F1, motivated by the structure of temporal constraints. UzZaman & Allen propose “temporal evaluation” using temporal closure to reward relations that are logically implied (equivalent but distinct edge sets) and argue that traditional methods miss these equivalences and fragment evaluation across subtasks. [36] QA TempEval pushes further by re-centering evaluation on temporal QA performance rather than edge-level scoring, arguing it better reflects whether extracted temporal information supports an end-user task. [13] More recently, tieval operationalizes this multi-corpus, multi-metric reality by standardizing corpora access and providing domain-specific operations (like temporal closure) and temporal awareness metrics, explicitly targeting comparability across datasets with incompatible formats and annotation schemes. [37]
Causality evaluation in EKG contexts is often borrowed from causality identification tasks over text, using specialized corpora. Mirza & Tonelli’s work provides a TimeML-inspired annotation framework and a language resource capturing temporal and causal relations, evaluating extraction systems over those annotations. [14] The Event StoryLine Corpus explicitly targets story-level temporal and causal relation detection across document collections. [15] MECI and FinCausal 2020 demonstrate that causality evaluation is expanding toward multilingual and domain-specific settings, but these efforts remain largely separated from holistic EKG evaluation frameworks that connect causal edge correctness to graph-level inference behavior. [38]
Graph-structure-level evaluation appears in event graph research but is not widely adopted in EKG practice. Glavaš & Šnajder’s event graph work proposes overall quality metrics based on tensor products between automatically and manually constructed graphs, moving toward holistic structural similarity rather than edge-by-edge scoring. [39] In 2026, STAGE’s Event-Structure Consistency metric similarly prioritizes structural coherence and faithful abstraction over exact matching, explicitly acknowledging that event summaries should be evaluated non-symmetrically (coverage + faithfulness). [17]
Finally, “integration and use-case demonstration” is a recurring evaluation style in cross-lingual/open analytics EKGs. OEKG emphasizes integration pipeline robustness and demonstrates usability through use cases such as hybrid QA and language-specific event recommendation, reflecting a pragmatic, application-driven evaluation lens rather than a standardized metric suite. [9] ChronoGrapher similarly evaluates utility through a user study on event-centric prompting for question answering, reporting improved groundedness of answers when prompts are enriched with event-centric KG triples compared to generic KG triples or base prompts. [40]
Persistent gaps and methodological fault lines
The literature strongly suggests that EKG evaluation is fragmented: different projects validate different slices of “EKG-ness” (times/locations, extraction validity, QA utility, causal relation accuracy), often with incompatible benchmarks and metrics. The temporal IE community’s experience is instructive here: tieval explicitly attributes limited comparability to heterogeneous annotation schemes, inconsistent formats, and divergent metrics across datasets—conditions that are magnified in EKGs because EKGs combine multiple subtasks (event detection, argument extraction, temporal grounding, causal inference, cross-document linking). [41]
A recurring bottleneck is the scarcity of gold standards at EKG scope. NewsReader-style ECKG evaluation highlights that when no proper gold standard exists, evaluation falls back to human judgment over small samples, which provides useful spot checks but does not fully characterize graph-level reasoning behavior under complex multi-hop queries. [42] Even in large integrated resources like EventKG, evaluation focuses on specific pipeline steps (event identification, time fusion, location fusion) and attribute-level precision/coverage, leaving causal correctness, event evolution structure, and inference robustness largely outside the measured space. [43]
Causality is an especially important and difficult gap. Causal relations are often implicit, entangled with temporal order, and domain dependent; dedicated corpora such as Causal-TimeBank, ESC, and newer multilingual datasets like MECI help measure causal edge extraction, but do not by themselves validate whether an EKG’s causal subgraph supports reliable downstream inference (e.g., counterfactual queries, intervention-like reasoning, or causal chain explanations). [44] This is not merely an annotation issue: temporal evaluation work shows that logically equivalent structures can lead to misleading scores if evaluation does not account for closure and global consistency—an argument that applies even more strongly to causal chains, where multi-hop implications matter. [45]
Event granularity and contextual richness are often acknowledged but rarely standardized in evaluation. SEM’s design motivation includes representing “who/what/when/where” with flexibility and minimal commitment, but that flexibility means that “completeness” depends on intended queries and domain expectations; likewise, EventKG reports temporal and spatial coverage, but “contextual completeness” would also encompass participant roles, event types, provenance, and viewpoint-dependent descriptions, which are not uniformly measured. [46] Work that brings narrative or viewpoint into event-centric representations (e.g., narrative aspects and viewpoint-dependent querying) reinforces that semantic richness is not reducible to a fixed triple checklist. [47]
Multilingual and cross-domain robustness remains difficult to evaluate holistically. OEKG assembles multilingual resources and demonstrates cross-lingual use cases, Event-QA provides multilingual verbalizations for event-centric QA, and MECI targets multilingual causality—yet these are typically evaluated in their own silos (QA accuracy, use-case demonstration, or causality F1) rather than under a unified EKG quality model that jointly measures cross-lingual alignment, temporal/causal consistency, and query utility. [48]
Finally, the “evolving” nature of event knowledge is underrepresented in evaluation protocols. Surveys of KG evolution explicitly distinguish static, dynamic, temporal, and event KGs and emphasize that timeliness and continuous updates are core to modern KGs; yet many evaluation studies remain one-off snapshots rather than continuous monitoring with incremental re-evaluation. [49] Sampling-based incremental evaluation methods for evolving KGs exist in the general KG literature, but are not commonly integrated into EKG evaluation toolchains. [50]
Design implications for a comprehensive EKG evaluation framework
A synthesis of the literature suggests that a comprehensive EKG evaluation framework must be explicitly multi-layered: it should combine (a) graph-intrinsic quality assessment, (b) event-specific semantic/temporal/causal validation, and (c) task-based utility evaluation—while remaining scalable under open-world, evolving conditions. [51]
At the foundational layer, automated constraint validation provides a repeatable baseline. SHACL-style constraints can formalize minimum schema expectations for events (e.g., required time anchors or allowed time representations, role/property cardinalities, datatype validity, and link patterns) and can be run continuously as the graph evolves. [22] Test-driven linked data quality assessment extends this by encouraging reusable “quality tests” as SPARQL templates, aligning well with EKG needs where many constraints are query-expressible (e.g., “events with start date must not have end date before start date,” “causal edge must not violate temporal order unless explicitly modeled,” “location must be a place-like node”). [23]
For correctness at scale, the literature supports sampling-centered auditing with statistical guarantees, especially for evolving graphs. Efficient KG accuracy evaluation methods provide concrete designs (e.g., stratified sampling and reservoir-style incremental approaches) that reduce human labeling costs while producing meaningful accuracy estimates, which can be adapted to EKG strata such as “high-impact events,” “recently updated events,” “causal edges,” or “cross-lingual links.” [50] This is a practical route to making “EKG evaluation” continuous rather than occasional—an important requirement given the rapid evolution of event knowledge. [49]
Temporal and causal evaluation should adopt graph-aware metrics rather than relying solely on edge matching. Temporal evaluation via closure demonstrates how to score equivalence classes of temporal structures, not just extracted tlinks, and how to handle global consistency—capabilities directly relevant to temporal subgraphs in EKGs. [45] QA-driven temporal evaluation (QA TempEval) suggests a complementary principle: evaluate whether temporal information supports answering relevant questions, rather than treating all annotated relations as equally important. [13] A comprehensive EKG framework can therefore combine (i) temporal-closure-aware structural scoring, (ii) QA-style temporal competency scoring, and (iii) time-attribute correctness checks (as done in EventKG time fusion evaluation), enabling both low-level and task-level insights. [52]
Causal evaluation should similarly connect edge quality to graph-level inference and application outcomes. Causal-TimeBank and ESC provide edge-level and storyline-level gold standards for temporal/causal relation detection; MECI and FinCausal extend coverage to multilingual and domain-specific causality. [53] An EKG evaluation framework that aims to measure “causal inference capability” needs to go beyond “does the causal edge exist?” toward “do causal chains support coherent explanations and avoid contradictions with temporal constraints?”, borrowing ideas from non-symmetric event-structure evaluation (coverage + faithfulness) and from temporal consistency enforcement. [54]
For contextual richness, the literature suggests separating “presence of context” from “usefulness of context.” SEM motivates flexible representation of actors/places/times, while EventKG reports location/time coverage; but task-centric benchmarks (Event-QA) and pipeline-driven ECKG evaluations show that context matters primarily insofar as it enables queries, timelines, and interpretation. [55] Practically, this points to dual metrics: (a) contextual completeness measures (coverage of roles, places, times, provenance) and (b) contextual utility measures (performance lift in event-centric QA, timeline generation, event recommendation, or domain-specific decision tasks). [56]
Extrinsic evaluation can be systematized using the broader KG community’s task-based frameworks while keeping EKG-specific tasks central. KGrEaT’s philosophy—evaluate KGs by downstream performance under fixed setups—can be adapted so that event-centric tasks (timeline generation, event QA, causal question answering, risk scenario queries) become first-class “task modules,” and so that comparisons span multiple EKG models under shared benchmarks. [57] Recent EKG construction and integration works (OEKG and ChronoGrapher) show how “use case utility” is often demonstrated today; formalizing those demonstrations into standardized task suites and reporting protocols would directly address the fragmentation highlighted across the literature. [58]
Finally, metric validation and trustworthiness of the evaluation itself must be treated as a first-class concern. The KG quality literature emphasizes “fit for purpose” and balancing comprehensiveness with practicality; temporal evaluation work highlights pitfalls of traditional metrics; and recent LLM-based evaluation frameworks demonstrate both promise and risk (e.g., dependence on model judgment, potential bias, and the need for human verification). [59] The emerging direction exemplified by STAGE—explicitly decomposing evaluation into interpretable dimensions (coverage, faithfulness) and providing detailed judging criteria—offers a template for validating new EKG metrics: require that each proposed metric (i) corresponds to a meaningful EKG capability, (ii) is measurable with acceptable cost, (iii) correlates with downstream utility where appropriate, and (iv) is robust under equivalent-but-distinct graph representations. [60]
 
